# A Printable Organic Electron Transport Layer for Low-Temperature-Processed, Hysteresis-Free, and Stable Planar Perovskite Solar Cells

## abstract
Despite recent breakthroughs in power conversion efficiencies (PCEs), which have resulted in PCEs exceeding 22%, perovskite solar cells (PSCs) still face serious drawbacks in terms of their printability, reliability, and stability. The most efficient PSC architecture, which is based on titanium dioxide as an electron transport layer, requires an extremely high-temperature sintering process (≈500 °C), reveals hysterical discrepancies in the device measurement, and suffers from performance degradation under light illumination. These drawbacks hamper the practical development of PSCs fabricated via a printing process on flexible plastic substrates. Herein, an innovative method to fabricate low-temperature-processed, hysteresis-free, and stable PSCs with a large area up to 1 cm2 is demonstrated using a versatile organic nanocomposite that combines an electron acceptor and a surface modifier. This nanocomposite forms an ideal, self-organized electron transport layer (ETL) via a spontaneous vertical phase separation, which leads to hysteresis-free, planar heterojunction PSCs with stabilized PCEs of over 18%. In addition, the organic nanocomposite concept is successfully applied to the printing process, resulting in a PCE of over 17% in PSCs with printed ETLs.

Despite recent breakthroughs in power conversion efficiencies (PCEs), which have resulted in PCEs exceeding $22\%$ , perovskite solar cells (PSCs) still face serious drawbacks in terms of their printability, reliability, and stability. The most efficient PSC architecture, which is based on titanium dioxide as an electron transport layer, requires an extremely high-temperature sintering process $\scriptstyle(\approx500^{\circ}\mathbf{C})$ , reveals hysterical discrepancies in the device measurement, and suffers from performance degradation under light illumination. These drawbacks hamper the practical development of PSCs fabricated via a printing process on flexible plastic substrates. Herein, an innovative method to fabricate low-temperature-processed, hysteresis-free, and stable PSCs with a large area up to $\mathsf{1c m}^{2}$ is demonstrated using a versatile organic nanocomposite that combines an electron acceptor and a surface modifier. This nanocomposite forms an ideal, self-organized electron transport layer (ETL) via a spontaneous vertical phase separation, which leads to hysteresis-free, planar heterojunction PSCs with stabilized PCEs of over $18\%$ . In addition, the organic nanocomposite concept is successfully applied to the printing process, resulting in a PCE of over $17\%$ in PSCs with printed ETLs.
Methylammonium lead trihalide perovskites are currently emerging as a new class of organic–inorganic hybrid semiconductors for use in ultraefficient solar harvesters.[1–5] Their striking photovoltaic feature comes from their intrinsic properties, including a high charge carrier mobility,[6,7] a low exciton binding energy,[8–11] a long exciton diffusion length,[12–14] a large absorption coefficient,[15,16] and a broad absorption spectrum.[17] Moreover, the solution processing of perovskite materials allows for compatibility with roll-to-roll printing processing, which facilitates cost-effective manufacturing and accelerates the practical development of perovskite solar cells (PSCs). This new photovoltaic technology led to an unprecedented increase in the power conversion efficiencies (PCEs) from $3.8\%$ to 22.1%.[18–20]
The majority of efficient PSCs, including certified devices from accredited laboratories, adopt a regular n-i-p structure consisting of titanium dioxide $\left(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\right)$ as the electron transport layer (ETL).[21–27] However, despite the significant improvements in device performance, the $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ - based PSCs still have serious weaknesses that must be overcome for successful commercialization. First, the sintering process required to create highly crystallized and compact $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ films requires temperatures too high $(\approx500~^{\circ}\mathrm{C})$ to be compatible with flexible plastic substrates and hinders their use in various transparent electrodes. Second, accurately assessing PCEs using current density–voltage $\left(J-V\right)$ characteristics in PSCs with a $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ layer is difficult because of a serious efficiency discrepancy: the so-called “hysteresis effect.”[28–31] The PCE value depends on the scanning conditions for the $J{-}V$ measurements, such as the scan direction, scan rate, light soaking, and biasing. This hysteretic $J{-}V$ behavior yields an over or underestimation of the PCEs and drastically reduces the reliability of the PSC efficiencies. Finally, the $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ layer results in poor device stability because the photocatalytic effect of $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ causes the perovskite layers to decompose via a photo-oxidation process under humid conditions and continuous light illumination.[32]
Organic ETLs are promising practical alternatives to $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ because of their beneficial features, such as a low-temperature process and hysteresis alleviation.[33–41] Recently, there have been research efforts to develop organic ETLs using polyelectrolyte/organic molecule bilayers[33–35] and vacuum-processed[36,37] or doped fullerene.[41] Although the organic ETLs have been successfully used to replace the $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ layer, they have disadvantages in terms of film formation because they require a vacuum process to deposit the fullerene, a long annealing time to activate the doping process, and delicate control of the ultrathin thickness $(<10\ \mathrm{nm})$ of polyelectrolytes. These disadvantages increase the complexity of the processing steps for the preparation of high-quality films and limit the application of organic ETLs in a continuous printing process. Therefore, developing an ETL that is printable and that simultaneously eliminates the hysteresis effect as a core function has been an important challenge.
Here, we present an innovative method to fabricate efficient and reliable PSCs with a regular structure by introducing selfassembled organic nanocomposites (SAONs) as a new ETL that employs n-type polyelectrolytes and fullerene derivatives. Using a spontaneous vertical phase separation driven by the surface energy difference of the SAON components, we succeeded in forming ideal, self-organized ETLs with dual functionality as an electron acceptor and surface work function modifier. By investigating the photovoltaic performance in combination with electrical and photophysical analysis techniques, we found that efficient charge transfer at the ETL/perovskite interface plays an important role in determining the efficiency, hysteresis, and stability. As a result, we successfully fabricated hysteresisfree PSCs that exhibit stabilized device operation with a PCE of $18.1\%$ using the self-assembled ETL concept. More importantly, a PCE of $17.3\%$ in PSCs with printed SAON films was achieved.
A schematic illustration of our device architecture and the corresponding energy level diagram are presented in Figure 1a,b. We introduced polyethyleneimine (PEI) as a surface work function modifier and [6,6]-phenyl $\mathrm{C}_{61}$ -butyric acid methyl ester (PCBM) as an electron acceptor, and they were combined into an SAON for a new and efficient ETL.[42,43] The SAON (also referred to as “PEI:PCBM”) solutions were prepared by mixing a small amount of the PEI solution with the PCBM solutions without the PEI rigorously inducing a doping effect in the PCBM (Figure S1, Supporting Information). The PEI:PCBM solutions were coated on an indium tin oxide (ITO) cathode via low-temperature solution processing. The PEI:PCBM SAON can function as an efficient ETL via the following two processes: (i) efficient electron transfer from the perovskite to the PCBM top layer due to the strong electron affinity of the PCBM and (ii) efficient electron collection from the PCBM to the ITO cathode by creating an interfacial PEI dipole bottom layer that reduces the work function of the cathode and forms a desirable ohmic contact between the lowest unoccupied molecular orbital (LUMO) level of the PCBM and the reduced work function of the cathode (Figure 1b).
The $\mathrm{CH_{3}N H_{3}P b I_{3}}$ layer was grown on the SAON film using a one-step solvent engineering method, which resulted in a smooth and compact film morphology with highly crystallized grains that were $300\ \mathrm{nm}$ as confirmed by the X-ray diffraction (XRD) pattern and high-resolution transmission electron microscopy (TEM) images (Figures S2 and S3, Supporting Information). For efficient hole collection, polytriarylamine (PTAA) and molybdenum oxide $(\mathrm{MoO}_{3})/\mathrm{Ag}$ were deposited on the perovskite layer. Therefore, our devices are based on planar heterojunction PSCs with a regular structure of $\mathrm{ITO}/$ PEI:PCBM $\mathrm{/CH_{3}N H_{3}P b I_{3}/P T A A/M o O_{3}/A g}$ , which is a printable device architecture.
The most remarkable feature of our work is that we introduced a new ETL concept using a vertical self-assembly of organic nanocomposites to fabricate high-efficiency PSCs. To emphasize the advantage of our SAON concept compared to the existing ETLs, we printed the SAON films using a doctor blade coating method (Figure 1c). When depositing the PEI:PCBM solution on the ITO surface, it is expected that the hydrophilic PEI molecules migrate toward the hydrophilic ITO surface, and the hydrophobic PCBM molecules aggregate on the air surface above via a spontaneous vertical phase separation to minimize the total system energy and maintain a thermodynamic equilibrium.[44] Subsequently, the migrated PEI molecules electrostatically self-assembled on the ITO surface. As a result, we can create a self-organized PEI/PCBM bilayer via one-step solution processing of the PEI:PCBM organic nanocomposite (Figure 1d). The printed SAON film is comparably uniform and smooth to the spin-coated SAON film without any severe
PCBM aggregation induced by the PEI additive (Figure S4, Supporting Information).
To verify our assumption, we investigated the vertical chemical distribution of the spin-coated and printed PEI:PCBM film by performing time-of-flight secondary-ion mass spectrometry (TOFSIMS). As shown in Figure 2a, both PEI:PCBM films exhibited a sharp mass spectra of the $\mathrm{CN^{-}}$ ions (PEI) near the ITO region, while the PEI peak intensities within the PCBM layers were extremely low. Moreover, the spectral shape of the PEI:PCBM film was similar to that of the PEI/PCBM bilayer (Figure S5, Supporting Information). This result indicated that the PEI/PCBM bilayer was properly self-organized through the spontaneous vertical phase separation of the PEI:PCBM. In addition, we examined whether the self-assembled PEI could effectively tune the work function of the ITO cathode using ultraviolet photoelectron spectroscopy (UPS) and Kelvin probe (KP) measurements. The ITO/PEI:PCBM sample rinsed with chlorobenzene (CB) exhibited work function values of 3.75 and $4.00~\mathrm{eV}$ in UPS and KP, respectively, and these values are comparable to those of the ITO/ PEI sample (Figure S6, Supporting Information).
To explore the impact of our PEI:PCBM film as an ETL on device operation, we fabricated a regular PSC with an $\mathrm{ITO}/$ PEI:PCBM/ $\mathrm{\Delta^{\prime}C H_{3}N H_{3}P b I_{3}/P T A A/M o O_{3}/A g}$ structure. We also fabricated two types of reference PSCs with ITO/PEI/ $\mathrm{CH_{3}N H_{3}P b I_{3}/P T A A/M o O_{3}/A g}$ and $\mathrm{ITO/PCBM/CH_{3}N H_{3}P b I_{3}}/$ PTAA/ $\mathrm{{'MoO}_{3}/A g}$ structures to clarify the role of each component in the SAON. The $J{-}V$ characteristics of the PSCs were measured under the standard air mass of 1.5 global $(\mathsf{A M}1.5\mathrm{G})$ with simulated solar irradiation at an intensity of $100\ensuremath{\mathrm{~mw~cm}^{-2}}$ . Figure 2b presents the $J{-}V$ characteristics of the PSCs with different ETLs. The PEI-based reference device produced poor and hysteretic $J{-}V$ characteristics because the insulating PEI layer has almost no electron accepting ability (Figure S7, Supporting Information). Although the reference cell using PCBM as an electron acceptor showed a relatively stable device operation with a slightly improved efficiency, it still displayed the problematic S-kink $J{-}V$ shape due to poor electron collection resulting from the large energy barrier (height $\Delta$ of ${\approx}0.8\ \mathrm{eV}$ ) between the LUMO $(4.0\ \mathrm{eV})$ of the PCBM and the work function $(4.8~\mathrm{eV})$ of the ITO cathode. In contrast, using the advantages of both the PEI (ohmic contact) and PCBM (electron transfer), the PEI:PCBM-based control device yielded a stabilized and reproducible device operation with a high PCE of $18.1\%$ , an opencircuit voltage $(V_{\mathrm{oc}})$ of $1.10~\mathrm{V},$ a short-circuit current $(J_{\mathrm{sc}})$ of $20.73\mathrm{\mA\cm^{-2}}$ , and a fill factor (FF) of 0.79 (Figure 2b). More importantly, the PSC using the printed PEI:PCBM ETL showed excellent photovoltaic parameters, with a PCE of $17.3\%$ , a $V_{\mathrm{oc}}$ of $1.09\mathrm{V},$ a $J_{\mathrm{sc}}$ of $20.42\mathrm{\mA\cm^{-2}}$ , and a FF of 0.77, which are comparable to those of the device using the PEI/PCBM bilayer (Figure 2b and Figure S8, Supporting Information). We confirmed that the vertical self-assembly of PEI and PCBM is highly reproducible in the printed SAON films, as shown in Figure 2c. The PSCs with the doctor blade-coated PEI:PCBM films yielded reproducible and similar performances to the spin-coated PEI:PCBM device, which indicates that mixed organic molecules can self-organize during the printing process and the low-temperature solution process SAON concept is compatible with the continuous printing process. One of the devices (active area of $0.1\mathrm{cm}^{2}.$ ) exhibiting high PCE was tested by an independent solar cell calibration laboratory (Korea Institute of Energy Research) for certification, which confirmed a PCE of $17.4\%$ , with $V_{\mathrm{oc}}=1.131\mathrm{~V},$ $J_{\mathrm{sc}}=19.63~\mathrm{mA}~\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ , and $\mathrm{FF}=0.783$ (Figure 2d).
To obtain further insight into the interface engineering in the regular PSCs using the SAON film, we introduced two additional ETLs, PEI:indene- $\mathrm{\DeltaC_{60}}$ bisadduct (ICBA) and planar $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ , and fabricated PSCs with an active area of $1\ \mathrm{cm}^{2}$ for standard photovoltaic characterization. Figure 3a presents the $J{-}V$ characteristics of the large area devices using PEI:PCBM, PEI:ICBA, and $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ . The details of the photovoltaic parameters are presented in Table S1 (Supporting Information). Both reference PSCs exhibited inferior performances compared to the control device and revealed a severe hysterical discrepancy in the $J{-}V$ characteristics between the reverse and forward scan directions. It should be noted that the poor device performance of the PEI:ICBA device is ascribed to the energy barrier (height $\Delta$ of ${\approx}0.1$ eV) between the LUMO level of ICBA $(3.8~\mathrm{eV})$ and the conduction band of perovskite $(3.9\ \mathrm{eV})$ . We confirmed that the hysteresis behavior becomes more severe with an increase in the active area of the PSC and the light intensity, which is a serious bottleneck that will restrict future commercialization (Figure 3b and Figures S9–S11, Supporting Information). In contrast, no hysteresis was observed in the large-area PSC with PEI:PCBM ETL. The electrical and photophysical analyses for the ETLs are shown and discussed in the Supporting
Information (Figures S12–S14, and Table S2, Supporting Information). Additionally, we checked that all the SAON-based PSCs using different hole transport layers (HTLs) showed fully stabilized PCEs with respect to the scan directions and scan rates (Figure 3c and Figures S15–S19 and Table S3, Supporting Information). These results demonstrate that choosing efficient ETLs is critical to eliminating hysteresis in the PSCs and that our SAON is an outstanding ETL.
In addition to their printability and the elimination of hysteresis, our SAONs offer another important advantage related to the stability of PSCs against self-/photodegradation and photo-oxidation. Because conventional metals (e.g., Al and Ag) are highly unstable in PSC technology, we used stable Au as the top electrode (Figure S20, Supporting Information) to exclude the degradation effects originating from the top metal contacts. A stability test to evaluate the self-degradation of the PSCs was performed in ambient air. As shown in Figure 4a, the control devices with an SAON layer survived for a longer period of time, whereas the $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ ETL resulted in the rapid degradation of the PSC performance within a few days. This finding is attributed to the hydrophobicity of the SAONs, which helps inhibit the intrusion of moisture into the perovskite, improving the air stability of the PSC. To gain a deeper understanding of the photodegradation behavior of the PSCs, we also measured the PCE decay profiles of the PSCs in an $\Nu_{2}$ -filled glove box under continuous light exposure with an irradiation intensity of $100\mathrm{mW}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ and a simulated AM 1.5G spectrum. Under light illumination, the PCEs of the reference devices with PEI:ICBA and $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ considerably decreased, whereas a significant enhancement in the photostability was observed for the PSCs with PEI:PCBM, which retained ${\approx}90\%$ of their initial PCE for up to $60\mathrm{~h~}$ (Figure 4b). Additionally, by replacing methylammonium iodide by much more stable cesium iodide,[45] we found that PSCs with $\mathrm{Cs}_{0.05}(\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{NH}_{3})_{0.95}\mathrm{PbI}_{3}$ exhibit prolonged lifetime (Figure S21, Supporting Information). We believe that introduction of the formamidinium cation would be able to further increase the device stability. Little photodegradation was observed to be associated with the degradation of the perovskites because none of the PSCs exhibited changes in the color of the perovskite active layers. These results imply that the invisible accumulation of electrons at the ETL/perovskite interfaces results in the unwanted photodegradation of PSCs. The charge accumulation might become severe, which hinder the formation of stable electric field distribution within the devices, presumably resulting in much faster degradation in the PEI:ICBAbased devices.[46] However, we believe that further studies should be needed to address this issue. Furthermore, we also conducted a stability test to investigate the photo-oxidation of PSCs under humid conditions with continuous light illumination (Figure 4c). We confirmed that the $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ -based reference cell exhibited a rapid decrease in the PCE accompanied by a remarkable change in the color of the perovskite active layers, which presumably originated from the well-known photocatalytic effect of $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ that causes perovskite materials to decompose. In contrast, the PCE of the control device displayed a relatively gradual decrease, and there was no change in the color of the perovskite active layers. These results indicated that organic ETLs are photochemically inert, thereby enabling the fabrication of PSCs with long-term stability.
In this work, we demonstrated a promising solution for the problematic $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ layer in PSCs by inventing an innovative selfassembled organic ETL. The successful combination of the work function adjustability of n-type polyelectrolytes and the electron accepting property of fullerene derivatives in a versatile organic nanocomposite led to the development of a new class of organic ETLs that are stable, simple, and printable with a low-temperature process, which allowed us to fabricate hysteresis-free PSCs with stabilized PCEs of over $18\%$ . We believe that our approach will help to realize printed, large-area perovskite photovoltaics for commercial applications and provide scientific and technological inspiration to the organic–inorganic hybrid electronics fields.
## Experimental Section
Materials and Preparation: All of the materials were used as received. The ${\mathsf{C H}}_{3}{\mathsf{N H}}_{3}|_{3}$ was synthesized according to a method previously reported.[23] A 1.5 m $\mathsf{\Pi}_{1}\mathsf{C H}_{3}\mathsf{N H}_{3}\mathsf{P b l}_{3}$ solution was prepared by dissolving the synthesized ${\mathsf{C H}}_{3}{\mathsf{N H}}_{3}{\mathsf{P b l}}_{3}$ (Dyesol) and $\mathsf{P b l}_{2}$ (Alfa Aesar, $99.9985\%)$ in a molar ratio of 1:1 in $\gamma$ butyrolactone/dimethylsulfoxide $(7;3~\mathsf{v o l\%})$ . PEI (Aldrich, $50\mathrm{\textmu}\mathrm{wt}\%$ in $H_{2}O)$ was diluted to $0.05\ \mathrm{wt\%}$ in 2-methoxyethanol (Sigma-Aldrich, anhydrous, $99.8\%$ . All fullerene derivatives (FDs) were purchased from Nano-C. The PCBM and ICBA were dissolved in CB to create a $2\mathrm{\ut\%}$ solution. For the PEI:FD nanocomposite, the PEI and FD solutions were mixed at a 1:9 volume ratio. The titanium diisopropoxide bis(acetylacetonate) $175\mathrm{~\wt\%~}$ in isopropanol) was purchased from Aldrich. As HTLs, P3HT $(M_{\mathrm{w}}=50000\ g\ m\mathrm{ol^{-1}})$ ), PCDTBT $(M_{\mathrm{w}}=38~000~\mathrm{g}~\mathsf{m o l}^{-1})$ , and T1 were purchased from 1-Material. Spiro and PTAA $(M_{\mathrm{w}}=17~500~\mathrm{g}~\mathsf{m o l^{-1}})$ ) were purchased from Lumtec and EM Index, respectively. P3HT and T1 were dissolved in CB to create $1.5~\mathrm{wt\%}$ solutions. PCDTBT was dissolved in 1,2-dichlorobenzene (DCB) at $0.5\ \mathrm{wt\%}$ . The Spiro/CB $(70\mathrm{mg}\mathsf{m}^{-1}$ ) solution was prepared with $28.8\upmu\mathrm{L}$ of 4-tert-butylpyridine (tBP), $\rceil7.5~\upmu\upiota$ of Li-bis(trifluoromethanesulfonyl) imide (Li-TFSI)/acetonitrile $(520\mathrm{~mg~}\mathsf{m L}^{-1})$ , and $29~\upmu\upiota$ of tris(2-(1Hpyrazol-1-yl)-4-tert-butylpyridine)cobalt(III)bis(trifluoromethylsulphonyl) imide/acetonitrile $(300m g\ m L^{-1})$ . The PTAA/toluene $(70\ m g\ m L^{-1})$ solution was prepared with $7.5~\upmu\up L$ of Li-TFSI/acetonitrile $(770\mathrm{~mg\mL^{-1}};$ ) and $7.5~\upmu\up L$ of tBP/acetonitrile $(7:7\ \mathrm{vol}\%)$ .
Perovskite Solar Cell Fabrication and Measurements: All component layers were spin-coated in an ${\mathsf N}_{2}$ -filled glove box. The n-i-p PSCs were fabricated with a structure of $\mathsf{I T O/S A O N/C H_{3}N H_{3}P b l_{3}/H T L/M o O_{3}/}$ Ag. The precleaned, ITO-coated glass substrates were treated with UV–ozone before device fabrication. The SAON (PEI:FD) solution was spin-coated at 1200 rpm on the glass/ITO substrate. The resulting film thickness was $50\mathsf{n m}$ . The samples were dried under vacuum conditions. For fabrication of the $\mathsf{T i O}_{2}$ film, $0.75~\mathrm{~M~}$ titanium diisopropoxide bis(acetylacetonate) in butanol solution was spin-coated at 2000 rpm for $20~\mathsf{s}$ . The resulting film was baked at $125^{\circ}\mathsf C$ for $5\mathrm{\min}$ and then annealed at $500~^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ for $\rceil\textrm{h}$ in a furnace. Subsequently, the perovskite precursor solution was spin-coated onto the ITO/SAON and $\mathsf{I T O}/\mathsf{T i O}_{2}$ substrates via a consecutive three-step spin-coating process at 800, 3000, and 5000 rpm for $20\ s$ . During the spin-coating at 5000 rpm (after $5\ s_{\scriptscriptstyle{\perp}}^{\prime}$ ), the substrates were treated using the diethyl ether dropping process $(200-400~\upmu\up L)$ and then annealed at $\mathsf{l o o\mathsf{\Lambda}^{\circ}C}$ for $10\min$ . The resulting thickness of the ${\mathsf{C H}}_{3}{\mathsf{N H}}_{3}{\mathsf{P b l}}_{3}$ film was $\approx350$ nm. Various HTLs were tested to confirm the validity of the SAON concept. The P3HT, PCDTBT, T1, Spiro, and PTAA solutions were spin-coated onto ${\mathsf{C H}}_{3}{\mathsf{N H}}_{3}{\mathsf{P b l}}_{3}$ at 1500, 1500, 1500, 4000, and 3000 rpm, respectively. Finally, ${\mathsf{M o O}}_{3}$ $(5\ \mathsf{n m})/\mathsf{A g}\ (700\ \mathsf{n m})$ was deposited via thermal evaporation under a ${\mathsf{1}}\times{\mathsf{10^{-7}}}$ Torr vacuum. The $\mathsf{A}\mathsf{g}$ electrode area $(4.64~\mathsf{m m}^{2})$ defined the active area of the device. In the case of Spiro and PTAA-based PSCs, the samples were kept in ambient air for $\rceil\textrm{h}$ before measurement, which increased the device performance due to the oxygen doping of HTLs. The intensity of the solar spectrum obtained from the xenon (Xe) lamp (150 W Oriel) in the solar simulator was calibrated using a calibrated standard silicon solar cell certified by the National Renewable Energy Laboratory (NREL). The J–V characteristics of the devices were measured using a Keithley 236 Source Measure Unit (SMU) under air mass $\mathsf{1.5~C}$ global $({\mathsf{A M}}\ 1.5\ {\mathsf{C}})$ illumination with a $\mathsf{l}00\mathsf{m}\mathsf{W}\mathsf{c m}^{-2}$ irradiation intensity. The external quantum efficiency (EQE) spectra were measured using a solar cell spectral response/QE/EQE measurement system (PV Measurements, Inc.).
